f Gelston in 1799.","title":"Gelston Castle"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" However, Sir William Douglas died without issue in 1809 and his property was divided between various nieces and nephews, with Gelston going to the youngest daughter of his brother James, Mathilda Douglas.","title":"Gelston Castle"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 1813 Mathilda married William Maitland, and their family continued to preside over the estate for most of the 19th Century.","title":"Gelston Castle"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Gelston then became the property of the Galliers-Pratts who retained the castle for pheasant shooting, and from whom it was requisitioned during World War II.","title":"Gelston Castle"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" During this time it served as a home for handicapped boys evacuated from Glasgow, before the roof was removed once this use had ceased.","title":"Gelston Castle"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"One Night in Istanbul, also known as One Night in Istanbul: The Movie, is a British comedy-drama film, directed by James Marquand and produced/written by Nicky Allt.","title":"One Night in Istanbul"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film stars Steven Waddington, Paul Barber, Lucien Laviscount, Samantha Womack, and Ingvar Eggert Sigurðsson.","title":"One Night in Istanbul"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It is based on the play of the same name by Nicky Allt.","title":"One Night in Istanbul"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: The Bye Bye Man
